This page opens Section 4, 'Canonical topoi associated with a global stratification'. The programme is to show that a stratified space (or topos) gives rise to further canonical objects, and that these behave well under base change.

Part A, 'general inverse images', first recalls the axioms used so far for a stratification of X indexed by a poset I. There are four conditions, labelled (1):
- (a) each X_i is closed and the family (X_i) is locally finite;
- (b) i ≤ j implies X_i ⊂ X_j;
- (c) X is the union of the X_i;
- (d) for all i, j, X_i ∩ X_j is the union of the X_k with k ≤ i, j.
So i ↦ X_i is a closed-subset-valued filtration indexed by I, compatible with meets in the sense of (d). The open strata are X_i^* = X_i minus the union of the X_k with k < i.

For any X' → X, the pulled-back family X'_i := X_i ×_X X' (formula (2)) satisfies the same axioms. Grothendieck then observes that the semi-simplicial system of the X_{Δ_r} has, as inverse image, the analogous system X'_{Δ_r} built from the X'_i. Here Δ_r runs over chains i_0 < ... < i_r of I, i.e. simplices of the nerve of I. These X_{Δ_r} are the 'elementary topoi' (tubes, strata and links) of the previous page, out of which X is recovered as a 2-limit. This gives the isomorphisms (3):
- X'_{Δ_r} ≅ X_{Δ_r} ×_X X';
- X'^*_{Δ_r} ≅ X^*_{Δ_r} ×_X X';
- dot-X'_{Δ_r} ≅ dot-X_{Δ_r} ×_X X'.
The purpose is to make the whole stratified-gluing apparatus functorial under base change. That in turn should allow the canonical topoi of a stratification to be expressed through the inductive system of elementary topoi, as announced at the end of p. 22.
